Keep stable application instructions in a shared request builder and supply them for the first request and every continuation. Inspect a redacted outgoing payload, not just the conversation UI. The following deliberately text-only JavaScript example builds a request without sending it; choose a model available to your application and pass the actual prior response ID only for continuation.

```javascript
function buildResponseRequest({ model, instructions, input, previousResponseId }) {
  if (typeof model !== "string" || !model.trim()) {
    throw new TypeError("model is required");
  }
  if (typeof instructions !== "string" || !instructions.trim()) {
    throw new TypeError("stable instructions are required on every turn");
  }
  if (typeof input !== "string" || !input.trim()) {
    throw new TypeError("this text-only example requires input");
  }
  const request = { model, instructions, input };
  if (previousResponseId !== undefined) {
    if (typeof previousResponseId !== "string" || !previousResponseId.trim()) {
      throw new TypeError("previousResponseId must be a nonempty response ID");
    }
    request.previous_response_id = previousResponseId;
  }
  return request; // Build only: no SDK call, credentials, or network.
}
```

Use the returned object with your existing SDK transport. The builder cannot validate ownership, retention or existence of a response ID. It also does not establish that a model obeyed a policy. Do not combine `previous_response_id` and `conversation`; the SDK guide describes them as mutually exclusive. If instead managing history manually, preserve required ordered output items, including reasoning/tool items rather than only assistant text.

The inspected Node SDK is v7.43.0, [released on 2026-10-22](https://github.com/openai/openai-node/releases/tag/v7.43.0). This is the reviewed documentation version, not the release that introduced instruction semantics or a claimed bug-fix version. A retry, model switch or plan upgrade does not restore an omitted field.
